// SOS panic-recursion pin (design 172 unit 4): a COMPILER-RAISED panic reaches
// the console intact, through a reporter that cannot itself panic.
//
// `panic.saw` beside this file calls `panic(...)` outright. This one does not
// say the word: it takes an out-of-range `Vector` index, so the message is
// assembled by the compiler's own trap path — the path that would recurse if
// the writer under it were not check-free. What is under test:
//
//   1. The reporter is reached from the TRAP path, not from a hand-written
//      call, so the whole design-137 stack-scratch assembly runs.
//   2. The message arrives INTACT — the `panic at ` prefix, the location and
//      the reason, three independent pieces — rather than truncated or
//      interleaved, which is what a second panic raised mid-report produces.
//      The location is `vector.saw`, not this file: design 122 reports the
//      TRAPPING expression's own line, and for an indexed accessor that is
//      inside std.
//   3. The machine stops NON-ZERO rather than hanging, which is what a
//      reporter that re-entered itself would do.
//
// The guarantee that makes (2) and (3) hold is not this test — it is that
// `hal.rt_write` and `hal.console_byte` contain no bounds check, no allocation
// and no checked arithmetic, verified from emitted IR (DF-172b). This test is
// what notices if that ever stops being true, on a real machine, on both
// profiles.
//
// The kernel core is imported for its `ktrap`, which the HAL's trap entry
// calls: every SOS image links the same trap handler even when this one never
// reaches it.

import kcore

@export("kmain")
func kmain() {
    // Built at run time so the index cannot be folded into a compile error.
    var v = Vector<Int>()
    v.push(11)
    v.push(22)

    // Out of range: the compiler's bounds check raises the panic, and its
    // message is assembled in stack scratch and handed to the seam.
    let n = v[v.len() + 5]

    // Never reached. Consuming `n` keeps the read from being optimized away;
    // if it somehow succeeded the harness sees a hang, which is also a failure.
    kcore.console().write_hex(n as UInt)
    while true { }
}
